update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('7354eaa5-1294-49df-ab0f-3458e9be7389'::uuid,'a8df74d4e3c1598e7fe7b419b4259fa3','The paragraph only counts musicians who play popular music professionally. Amateurs never come up, so we have no information about whether their numbers rose, fell, or stayed the same.'),
('962b0b9d-9835-47ba-9e0a-02e0921495bc'::uuid,'febd2ef5b90fcf03ac163ea00160288e','The paragraph says instruments were electrified. It never says whether any musician can play a nonelectric instrument, let alone most of them. A claim about musicians'' skills has nothing to stand on here.'),
('d91af5c4-50d1-42f6-a8d5-9de7ed44243e'::uuid,'a309023ba27a9c0e7ef76b6afa929aab','This is one way to spread musicians across more bands, but nothing in the paragraph says anyone plays in more than one band. The math only forces more bands; it does not tell us how musicians are spread across them. Every musician could play in exactly one band and the facts still hold.'),
('9c0ce691-0918-4027-bfba-0f5ea6a58ebc'::uuid,'01d4c48b2758d3e1d13163c448a3fd1f','"Required" means no one gets adequate deep sleep without afternoon exercise. The study only shows that exercisers got more deep sleep than a group that exercised little or not at all. And the mechanism points the other way: if heat is the cause, other sources of heat could do the job without exercise.'),
('1ebea67e-4423-4cf3-aa35-c5e3edd1e656'::uuid,'6268c37712c42a54c672f6ef4f46375b','The mechanism depends on body heat lasting until after bedtime. Morning exercise never comes up, and its heat could easily wear off long before night. Nothing lets us compare morning exercise with afternoon exercise.'),
('db0dc54b-4fa7-444a-8f01-1804375a5c5d'::uuid,'a61e3cee6299c2a5d3d18a78e07f95ac','"The best way" needs a comparison with every other way to get deep sleep. The researcher describes one thing that helps. That supports "this works," not "nothing works better."'),
('5eb2188f-1b6a-4a72-ada2-eb0666580b82'::uuid,'f9b855cd76ee0307f443d6de6fffc141','The researcher says deeper sleep comes from "this extra heat," a slight rise in body temperature that lasts past bedtime. A warm bath just before bed produces that same condition, so the mechanism predicts it should help too. Notice the move: when a stimulus names the mechanism behind a result, the supported answer often applies that mechanism to a new case. "Will likely" is as strong as the mechanism allows, and no stronger.'),
('397c754d-1182-4f18-b1ee-75711bf8d847'::uuid,'2efd92c19c1cac57841e8c21eaff3fa6','Lacking a test is not evidence against a claim. The linguist says we may not be able to check the hypothesis, which leaves it unconfirmed, not probably false. If we cannot test something, we have no grounds to call it likely true or likely false.'),
('99ee3604-856f-40bc-bdef-b640efc8cd6d'::uuid,'ee2f6b4fe7b7869c20790355ad384845','The linguist compares this one hypothesis with physical science. That tells us physical-science hypotheses are verifiable, not that they are the only verifiable ones. Plenty of hypotheses outside physical science could be testable; the passage just never discusses them.'),
('6cfc6704-6b7f-4152-9d85-d92a1cf9e41c'::uuid,'924b82c414c9871af87cd8c6776bc77b','This turns a fact about the hypothesis into a rule about what we should take seriously. The linguist never says what deserves serious consideration, so nothing here supports dismissing unverifiable hypotheses.'),
('3ebae480-2210-4a95-a928-827b5f6fafa9'::uuid,'114632d6222c5c8d190462f93319c0ab','If it is not clear the hypothesis could even be tested, then it has not been confirmed, and we have no reliable way to tell whether it is true. That leaves us exactly where D says: we do not know. Notice how little D claims. It takes the linguist''s point about testing one small step, which is what a supported answer usually does.'),
('025294d2-9cc7-496f-8d67-9b1abfdbf33f'::uuid,'698428eb1c27f55c1acb46d9d1ae82fd','Two problems. Like C, it adds a rule about what deserves to be taken seriously, which the linguist never states. And like B, it says "only" physical science, when the passage compares one hypothesis with physical science and says nothing about any other field.'),
('8220c83c-c7af-4f32-a64a-1b18a520b482'::uuid,'8988cdaef5adf28af61f949854b9ec75','The commentator says outright, "It is true that editors like to run antienvironmental pieces mainly because they seem to challenge the political orthodoxy." That is Winslow''s claim about editors, confirmed. The "But" that follows attacks the articles, not Winslow''s account of editors. The move to repeat: when a speaker concedes part of someone''s view and then pivots, sort which part was conceded. The conceded part is safe ground for a Most Supported answer.'),
('e5dc97e8-3971-4ead-adb5-2c0e1ffd4858'::uuid,'a58205eff998e12ba0452dba58c706a1','The commentator concedes the opposite: antienvironmentalists "can hardly claim to be dissidents, however much they may have succeeded in selling themselves as renegades." That phrasing grants, or at least allows, that the self-promotion worked. The commentator''s complaint is that the image is false, not that it failed to sell.'),
('339b8df5-1dad-4542-a389-3dd84be18520'::uuid,'44fd9f270a5b26c16ba9a0b7bacaec4f','This is the most tempting trap, because the commentator does push back on something. But the commentator agrees that editors run these pieces mainly because they seem to challenge orthodoxy, and that preference fits articles appearing regularly. So Winslow''s explanation does account for the frequency. What the commentator rejects is the idea that the pieces really are daring, which is a different claim from why editors publish them.'),
('1e835550-0977-4074-b736-31305a031805'::uuid,'a58205eff998e12ba0452dba58c706a1','The commentator says the reverse: "serious environmentalism is by no means politically orthodox." Environmentalism is the position the critics attack, so the attacked position is not the prevailing one. That is exactly why the critics are not real dissidents.'),
('e17f4d50-96b1-4168-bd49-baaa878c30a1'::uuid,'bc6e603214bbf93acea16c77f67836f8','The commentator describes the present: what editors prefer and where environmentalism stands now. Nothing is said about where environmentalism is headed, so a prediction that it will "eventually become" the prevailing position has no support.'),
('3a52b47a-b2f8-447f-9c58-a17a5fbd84f0'::uuid,'c83304d38b35f12e57fbb8002b648e50','The only thing we hear about whale behavior around engines is that they "do not seem to behave differently." If anything, that cuts against B, since attempting less communication would be a change in behavior. The premises are about harm to hearing, not about choosing to stay quiet.'),
('72aaacd6-11ba-4940-b6e3-8a114451fec2'::uuid,'93e79350d3174acfacae48dc16846258','Both premises meet here. The noise overlaps the frequencies the whales communicate in, and it can damage their hearing. A whale that hears worse in the very range it uses to talk can have trouble communicating. Notice the soft wording: "may impair." The premises say damage "can" happen, so a conclusion that claims only possibility is exactly as strong as the evidence. On a completion question, look for the answer that joins every premise and claims no more than they allow.'),
('9b34258e-4710-4eaf-b47c-4b21ba20e83d'::uuid,'d619c4f7bfde1d7e155907aa17256185','The passage never says where whales prefer to be. And it reports that whales do not seem to behave differently around engines, which gives us no sign that they seek out or avoid boat traffic at all.'),
('d887952e-572b-4284-8001-a85085e3cad1'::uuid,'04bb98c6e9d6db9f989922724e1025af','Food never comes up. To get here we would need to know the whales use hearing to hunt, and the passage only ties their hearing to communication. "Would probably be more successful" also claims an effect the premises never measure.'),
('3c9d564a-c848-4fa0-957c-8db577cc0642'::uuid,'bac8d8429a3d9f1929b03d6876c9b864','The passage tells us where early urban societies could be maintained, not where most people lived. If most early peoples lived in small rural groups that foraged, they could have lived anywhere. A claim about the whole population needs facts about the whole population.'),
('dd6262be-992e-4aa7-93d1-b893ed94575b'::uuid,'624490f2fa7bad795281d6a56c3d9d4c','Two problems. First, B is about all societies, but the passage says only that urban ones need large-scale farming; a small foraging society far from water breaks no rule in the passage. Second, B has no time limit, while the passage says irrigation became feasible far from water in more recent times. A modern society far from rivers could farm with irrigation, so B''s "only if" requirement does not follow.'),
('a820170c-7235-4d23-887b-828b3b9700cd'::uuid,'2599990c9fede990e0d42db84e9ec444','This follows from combining the three conditional statements, read in early times.

Far from rivers or lakes → no irrigation
No irrigation → no large-scale farming
No large-scale farming → no urban society
So: far from rivers or lakes → no urban society

The second and third lines are contrapositives of the rules in the stimulus. When a Most Supported paragraph hands you several conditionals that share terms, link them and look for the answer that joins the two ends.'),
('da216dbe-f0d1-40d9-b338-aeca5cb51e3f'::uuid,'01e8c2a054fb262f6c7b3dd4a51a5541','The passage points the other way. Early urban societies required large-scale farming, and large-scale farming requires irrigation.

Early urban society → large-scale farming → irrigation

Being near a river or lake makes irrigation possible; it does not remove the need for it. So D is contradicted, not supported.'),
('d6ed2603-9f8e-4766-9559-d4120a6e01e8'::uuid,'ae0b71cf38effc6028c839ec56b08c24','Rural societies never come up. The passage says foraging cannot feed dense urban populations, but it says nothing about how much rural societies foraged compared with how much they farmed.'),
('9bc2c618-d490-48e9-93fa-f12d25aae03f'::uuid,'481983aaf965d42e94036df3b41e8528','The passage links decomposition to ammonia smell, not to the percentage of organic matter. 80 percent tells us this is not the best compost, since the best is 40 to 60 percent, but nothing says high organic content means poor decomposition.'),
('78fae39e-101e-4beb-929f-0ef124884caa'::uuid,'0053e6447b2c666d923fed39892be0ed','The passage never says any compost harms soil. Compost under 40 percent organic matter and not dark brown fails the description of the best compost, so it is not the best. Not the best is a long way from "will make soil less fertile and will worsen soil drainage." It could still be good compost, or merely unhelpful.'),
('9439dc7f-76f2-4532-ac68-b4946cc7ee42'::uuid,'3cae24e9ef7cf9e773fceac928360c8b','This tries to prove a compost is good, and nothing in the passage gives us a way to prove that. The 40 to 60 percent figure is a requirement for the best compost, not a guarantee: best → 40 to 60 percent does not give us 40 to 60 percent → good. And sufficient decomposition only removes one reason to reject a compost. Clearing one obstacle does not make it good.'),
('5543b41a-98b9-487a-81e1-8896a6abaf3b'::uuid,'12b1edbb32c9483193c7463532f6f884','We only know that a strong ammonia smell means the organic matter is not sufficiently decomposed. "Sufficiently" is far short of "completely." The passage never describes the decomposition level of the best compost at all.'),
('b2448822-0874-41b4-b90f-a5fa28261b6c'::uuid,'5955df3abe1ab7175a8c2e000a3a561d','Chain the first and third statements. Strong ammonia smell → not appropriate for drainage and fertility. Contrapositive of the first rule: not appropriate → not good compost. So smelly compost is not good compost, whatever its color. The dark brown detail is bait: it is a feature of the best compost, but meeting a requirement never proves the label, and the ammonia smell rules the compost out anyway.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;